  "answer_text": "## What Are Opioids Derived From?\n\nOpioids are a class of drugs derived from substances that occur naturally in the **opium poppy** (*Papaver somniferum*) and are widely used for pain relief and sedation. There are three main categories:\n\n---\n\n### 1. \ud83c\udf3f Natural Opioids\nNatural opioids are substances derived directly from the opium poppy, typically from the **resin of the plant's seed pod**. They are made from naturally occurring plant compounds called **alkaloids** that come from the opium poppy. Natural opioids are also referred to as **opiates**.\n\nExamples include: **morphine, codeine, and opium**.\n\n---\n\n### 2. \ud83e\uddea Semi-Synthetic Opioids\nSemi-synthetic opioids are made by **chemically processing natural opioids**, modifying them into new opioid compounds. So, while these drugs are man-made and don't occur in nature, natural opioids from the opium poppy serve as the \"raw material\" that manufacturers use to make them.\n\nExamples include: **heroin, oxycodone, and hydrocodone**.\n\n---\n\n### 3. \u2697\ufe0f Synthetic Opioids\nSynthetic opioids are entirely man-made. Unlike semi-synthetic opioids, they are **not made from products of the opium poppy** and instead are made from other precursor chemicals. Synthetic opioid compounds are designed to **mimic natural opioids** (as well as endorphins), allowing them to activate opioid receptors.\n\nExamples include: **fentanyl, methadone, and tramadol**.\n\n---\n\n### A Brief History\nCultivation of the opium poppy for its active constituents is thought to have begun about **3,000 BCE**, and opioids that occur naturally in the plant have been used specifically for relieving pain and producing euphoria since at least the time of the ancient Greeks. In the 20th century, experiments with opiates led to the development of synthetic compounds with similar effects on the body. To distinguish them from natural opiates, these synthetic compounds were called **opioids** (meaning \"opiate-like\").",
